# 2026-08-18: the eight DOS games added to the asset base the same day. They were
# installed under /DOS but NOT given menu entries, and the Start menu is the ONLY
# launcher for a DOS binary in the shipping system - the terminal spawns ELF via
# sys_spawn_args and has no DOS path at all, so without these lines the games are
# present on disk and unreachable by any means. Entry points were read off the
# built image, not guessed: STUNTS ships LOAD.EXE (there is no STUNTS.EXE, only a
# STUNTS.COM loader stub), and it needs ARGUMENTS. MCGA selects the mode-13h
# driver; launched bare,
# LOAD.EXE relocates itself high, finds no driver name among ega/cga/tdy/mcga,
# prints "Invalid file name." and exits 1 (#172, MEASURED). The kernel splits
# a launch line on the first space in dos_launch_common(); before #172 only
# the DOSRUN.CFG path did, so no menu entry could pass an argument.
#
# THE `/ssb` FROM SETUP.DAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT PASSED, and this is the one
# line here that is a judgement rather than a transcription. Stunts' own
# SETUP.EXE wrote `load.exe /u MCGA  /ssb ` into /DOS/STUNTS/SETUP.DAT on
# this image, so /ssb is what the game was configured for. MEASURED, same
# kernel and same image, one run each, only the argument differing:
#   /u MCGA /ssb  -> loads, sets mode 13h, then spins in the OPL2 detection
#                    (`in al,0x218` -> 0xFF, `and al,0xE0 / cmp al,0xC0`,
#                    266 retries at 422a:0821), gives up and exit(1) at
#                    7,966,841 instructions with a black screen.
#   /u MCGA       -> TITLE SCREEN, then the attract-mode 3D drive, still
#                    running at the timeout with 58.8% frame motion.
# CORRECTED 2026-08-26 (#252). The sentence that used to be here said
# "MayteraOS emulates no AdLib/OPL2 and no Sound Blaster, so asking for one
# is asking for hardware that is not there". THAT HAS BEEN FALSE SINCE #181
# AND #182, and it was still being read as current three tickets later: an
# OPL2 answers at 0x388/0x389 and is synthesised for real in Ring 3
# (/APPS/FMSYNTH, userland/lib/opl2), and a Sound Blaster DSP with 8237 DMA
# answers at 0x220. Monkey Island's music was MEASURED coming out of the
# audio sink on 2026-08-26.
#
# THE #172 FAILURE ABOVE NO LONGER REPRODUCES. MEASURED 2026-08-26, golden
# 2215, /u MCGA /ssb, /CONFIG/DOSIO.CFG=2, one run: LOAD.EXE loads, runs, and
# is PASS/live at 42.3% frame motion at the timeout, with 23.2 s of audio at
# the sink. It does NOT exit 1 and it does NOT show a black screen.
#
# AND THE PORT IN THE #172 NOTE IS NOT THE PORT IT USES ANY MORE. The IOTRACE
# census for that run is 0x388 x114 and 0x389 x57, and NOT ONE access anywhere
# in 0x2xx. #172 saw `in al,0x218` because 0x388 answered 0xFF then, so Stunts
# fell through to hunting a Sound Blaster; now that #182 makes 0x388 answer,
# it stays on the AdLib path and never goes looking. Re-measure before quoting
# a port from an old trace: the port a guest uses is a CONSEQUENCE of what
# answered it last time.
#
# /ssb IS STILL NOT PASSED HERE, and this is now a QUALITY judgement rather
# than a "does it run" one. Under /ssb, Stunts writes FM registers far faster
# than the Ring-3 synthesiser drains them: MEASURED 799,836 pushed with
# 125,576 DROPPED and the ring pinned at 1024/1024 for the whole run. Its
# music therefore plays, badly. That is a #187-class ring problem at a write
# rate an order of magnitude above anything #187 measured, and it wants its
# own ticket and its own measurement.
#
# NOTE FOR WHOEVER TAKES THAT TICKET: none of this is caused by #252. The
# control arm was run - same image, same arguments, STOCK kernel - and it
# behaves the same (150,696 pushed, 120,160 dropped), which is what `i15w=0`
# on the [FMQ] line already said. Stunts was fixed by #182 and nobody had
# re-measured it since. Flip this entry to /ssb once the drop rate is fixed.
# STUNTS.COM loader stub), and KEEN7 is a Keen 4 MOD whose binary really is
# KEEN4E.EXE, which is why its label says so.
item: Aladdin | /DOS/ALADDIN/ALADDIN.EXE | icon=dosapp | type=dos
item: SkyRoads | /DOS/SKYROADS/SKYROADS.EXE | icon=dosapp | type=dos
item: The Secret of Monkey Island | /DOS/MONKEY/MONKEY.EXE | icon=dosapp | type=dos
item: Stunts | /DOS/STUNTS/LOAD.EXE /u MCGA | icon=dosapp | type=dos
item: Joust | /DOS/JOUST/JOUST.COM | icon=dosapp | type=dos
# 2026-08-28 (dosplay): THIS ENTRY HAD BEEN LAUNCHING THE UNPATCHED BINARY SINCE
# THE DAY IT WAS ADDED, and the owner reported it as "Keen 7 exits when I press
# space on the first screen".
#
# Commander Keen 7 is not a game, it is a PATCH. /DOS/KEEN7 ships CK4PATCH.EXE
# ("Commander Keen Patch-Loading Utility v0.9.0 beta"), KEEN7.PAT, CK4LOAD.BIN
# and a vanilla-layout KEEN4E.EXE, and its own Read Me says "To play, run
# Keen7.bat", whose entire 19-byte contents are `ck4patch KEEN7.PAT`. The
# patcher loads the executable itself, applies the mod's patches to the loaded
# image and runs it. Launching KEEN4E.EXE directly runs a VANILLA Keen 4 engine
# against Keen 7's MODDED data files.
#
# MEASURED, golden 2267, one run each, dosharness:
#   /DOS/KEEN7/KEEN4E.EXE          69 INT 21h calls, 0 unimplemented. It opens
#     GAMEMAPS.CK4, EGAGRAPH.CK4 and AUDIO.CK4 out of the KEEN7 directory, reads
#     the first EGAGRAPH chunks (to offset 150,144), and then
#     `[dos] DERAIL: zeros at cs:ip=048d:76ae ... dx=03ce insns=960000` - it
#     executes a run of 0x00 with the EGA graphics-controller port still in DX
#     and the guest exits 0 with only mode 03 ever set. It is not our input path
#     and it is not an INT 21h AH=4Ch quit: the engine jumps into nothing,
#     because a vanilla chunk table cannot decode a modded EGAGRAPH. Commander
#     Keen 4 (/GAMES/KEEN4) is the same engine and is fine, which is what
#     narrowed this to the mod rather than to the emulator.
#   /DOS/KEEN7/CK4PATCH.EXE KEEN7.PAT   98 INT 21h calls, and the launch line
#     below is CORRECT as far as it goes: the guest's CWD is seeded from the
#     executable's directory, so `4Eh findfirst dir='/DOS/KEEN7' pat='KEEN7.PAT'
#     -> hit` and `pat='KEEN4*.EXE' -> hit`, and it opens KEEN4E.EXE and reads
#     its MZ header. Then:
#         [int21:dos] AH=4b (unimplemented) ax=4b01 bx=062e cx=0000 dx=06c4
#     and it exits 0.
#
# SO THE REMAINING BLOCKER IS INT 21h AX=4B01h, "load program, do not execute" -
# the EXEC subfunction a patch-loader exists to use. It is unimplemented in this
# tree (a sibling established the same thing on The Dig and deliberately did not
# build it). Until it exists, NEITHER line runs the game; this one is the one
# that will run it the moment 4B01h lands, and it fails as a single named MISS
# instead of as an interpreter derail, which is the difference between a
# diagnosable gap and something that looks like an emulator bug.
#
# The entry is kept rather than removed because the Start menu is the ONLY
# launcher for a DOS binary in the shipping system (see the /GAMES.CFG note
# above): deleting the line would make the game unreachable by any means and
# would delete this explanation with it. The window title comes from the
# DIRECTORY, not from this label (dos_guest_title() takes seg[nseg-2]), so it
# shows as "KEEN7 (DOS)" either way.
item: Commander Keen 7 (Keen 4 mod) | /DOS/KEEN7/CK4PATCH.EXE KEEN7.PAT | icon=dosapp | type=dos
item: NetHack | /DOS/NETHACK/NETHACK.EXE | icon=dosapp | type=dos

# 2026-08-25 (#234f): SIMCITY CLASSIC. A plain 16-bit real-mode MZ, so it takes
# the x86_16 interpreter path and IS subject to the #232 CPU cap (the cap is
# forced off only for DOS/4GW guests, dosexec.c:8425). Entry point read off the
# staged tree, not guessed: SIMCITY.EXE is the game, SETTINGS.EXE is its video
# and sound configurator, and PICK.BAT is a DOS menu wrapper that needs
# CHOICE.EXE and a command interpreter, neither of which a MayteraOS DOS guest
# has. Launch the game directly.
#
# The window and taskbar title is DERIVED FROM THE DIRECTORY, not from this
# label: dos_guest_title() (dosexec.c:5943) takes seg[nseg-2] of the path and
# appends " (DOS)", so this shows as "SIMCITY (DOS)". Renaming the label here
# would not change it.
# THE /d2 IS LOAD-BEARING AND WAS MEASURED, NOT CHOSEN. It selects the game's
# MCGA/VGA 320x200x256 mode, INT 10h 0x13. The alternative, the vendor default
# EGA/VGA 640x480x16 (mode 0x12), renders every widget correctly and every GLYPH
# as a scatter of a few pixels, so the game is unreadable in it. The letters in
# the game's own usage string, SIMCITY [/d{EeCTHMm2}], are documented nowhere;
# all four of these were established by running it and reading which directory it
# opened and which mode it set: /dE -> CEGA/ + mode 0x12, /dC -> CGA/ (not
# shipped), /dM and /dm (case is folded) -> MONO/, /d2 -> MCGA/ + mode 0x13.
#
# The command tail reaches the guest through the #172 split in
# dos_launch_common(), the same mechanism the Stunts entry above uses.
item: SimCity Classic | /DOS/SIMCITY/SIMCITY.EXE /d2 | icon=dosapp | type=dos
# TWO ROGUES SHIP AND ONLY THE EMULATED ONE HAD AN ENTRY. userland/apps/rogue is
# the complete native BSD Rogue port: 15,004 lines across 38 .c files, built PIE
# against user-pie.ld, opening a real window via _win_create (curses.c:174) with
# its own cell renderer over SYS_WIN_DRAW_RECT/TEXT. It has been played and fixed
# at least once (curses.c:20-31 carries the SYS_WIN_CREATE outer-size note that
# cost Rogue its status row). Nothing launched it; the menu's "Rogue" was the DOS
# binary under the emulator.
#
# The native build is now the one called "Rogue": it is ours, it is a Ring-3 PIE,
# it gets a real window, and it pays no emulation cost. The DOS binary keeps an
# entry under a name that says what it is, because /DOS/ROGUE also earns its place
# as x86-16 test corpus (tools/x86-16-oracle uses it as a length-decoder oracle)
# and the two are not the same experience.
#
# Both are existence-checked by startmenu_model.rs::sm_model_finish(), so a
# missing path renders nothing rather than a launcher for nothing (#537).
item: Rogue | /APPS/rogue | icon=game
item: Rogue (DOS) | /DOS/ROGUE/ROGUE.EXE | icon=dosapp | type=dos

# 2026-08-21 (#207): DISCWORLD II. The owner asked "how do i start discworld
# from the imac i dont see it in the start menu OR in the file system?", and
# both halves of that were correct observations of one fact: Discworld II is
# not a file anywhere. It is a raw ISO 9660 volume in the UNPARTITIONED TAIL
# of the boot stick (#740), mounted at boot by usbvol_probe_and_mount() onto a
# DOS drive letter. Nothing that browses a filesystem can show it, and
# /APPS/DISKIMG cannot either: its scan looks for .iso/.img FILES inside
# DIRECTORIES, and a raw device volume is neither. The Start menu is the only
# launcher for a DOS binary in the shipping system, so with no line here the
# game was present on the medium and unreachable by any means.
#
# THE PATH IS THE CD's ROOT, NOT ITS DW2 SUBDIRECTORY. On a real Discworld II
# disc, DWB.EXE is at the root and \DW2 holds the game data plus VOLUME, the
# disc identity file. The game hardcodes that shape: it probes drives with INT
# 21h AX=4409h for one that answers "remote", chdirs it to \DW2 and opens
# VOLUME. MEASURED both ways on build 2008 (#207): from /WINDIR/DRIVE_E/DWB.EXE
# it sets VESA mode 0x101 and reaches the DISCWORLD II main menu; from a volume
# whose DW2 is the hard-disk INSTALL directory instead, the same binary gets
# `3Dh open FAIL '/WINDIR/DRIVE_E/DW2/VOLUME'`, prints "Discworld cannot locate
# the Discworld CD." and exits 15 after 92,970 instructions. The tail must hold
# the DISC images, not a DOSBox-style installed tree.
#
# WHY E: IS DERIVED AND NOT ASSUMED. usbvol mounts with DISKIMG_LETTER_AUTO,
# which is rustkern/drvmap.rs's lowest free letter of DRV_CLASS_CDROM; that
# file maps A:/B: to floppy, C: to fixed, D: reserved, E:..Z: to CD. At the
# point usbvol runs (kernel/main.c, right after diskimg_boot_harness()) no
# image is mounted on a normal boot, so the FIRST volume in the tail chain is
# always E: and the second is always F:. Write disc 1 first:
#   tools/usbvol/mkusbvol.sh --target <stick> --device --serial <serial> \
#       disc1.iso disc2.iso
# MEASURED: E: = disc 1 (label 20070601_14), F: = disc 2 (20070601_15); the
# game runs E:\DWB.EXE and takes its data from F:\DW2, which is why BOTH discs
# belong in the chain rather than only the one being launched.
#
# 2026-08-26 (no ticket, discentry) AMENDMENT, AND IT DECIDES WHETHER ANY VOLUME AFTER
# THIS ONE EXISTS AT ALL. The chain has no index: each volume's length is read
# from its OWN ISO 9660 primary volume descriptor, and usbvol_next_off_rs()
# probes align_up(offset + THAT declared length). mkusbvol.sh used to lay the
# next volume out at align_up(offset + the FILE's size), which differs the
# moment an ISO carries lead-out padding, as every ripped disc here does. Five
# volumes written, three mounted, no error anywhere: the kernel probed 1 MiB
# short of where volume 4 had been written, found no CD001 and stopped. Both
# sides now use the declared length and mkusbvol.sh re-walks the finished chain
# the way the kernel does before it reports success. Re-verified: the two
# Discworld discs still come up E: and F: with three more volumes behind them.
#
# AND IF THE STICK HAS NO DISCWORLD VOLUME THIS LINE COSTS NOTHING: the
# existence check in startmenu_model.rs::sm_model_finish() calls access() on
# the path, access() is stat(), and stat() has consulted the mounted image
# since #193. No volume means no E:, means no such path, means the item is
# silently dropped rather than rendered as a launcher for nothing (#537).
item: Discworld II | /WINDIR/DRIVE_E/DWB.EXE | icon=dosapp | type=dos

# 2026-08-25 (#234g): PYRO! 2.01, ON A MOUNTED 3.5" 720 KB FLOPPY IMAGE.
#
# This is the Discworld II shape one class down: the program is not a file on
# this filesystem at all, it is a file inside a disk image that /APPS/DISKIMG
# mounts on A:. /IMAGES/PYRO.IMG ships on the asset base; mounting it makes
# /WINDIR/DRIVE_A/INSTALL.EXE resolve, and this line then renders.
#
# AND IF NOTHING IS MOUNTED THIS LINE COSTS NOTHING, by the same mechanism the
# Discworld line relies on: sm_model_finish() in startmenu_model.rs calls
# access() on the path, fat_open() only takes its image branch when
# diskimg_is_mounted('A') is true (fs/fat.c:372), so with A: empty the path does
# not exist and the item is silently dropped rather than rendered as a launcher
# for nothing (#537). The model rebuilds about once a second, so the entry
# appears within a second of mounting and disappears on eject.
#
# WHY INSTALL.EXE AND NOT PYRO.EXE. There is no PYRO.EXE on this disk. The
# 720 KB image holds exactly three files, MEASURED by mounting it:
# INSTALL.EXE (38,112), PYROCOMP.EXE (288,105) and READ.ME (5,343). PYROCOMP.EXE
# is the compressed payload, and INSTALL.EXE is what unpacks it. Pointing this
# at a PYRO.EXE that does not exist would be the FONTBOOK phantom again.
#
# HONEST LIMIT, stated because it is a property of the medium and not a bug: a
# mounted image is READ-ONLY (DISKIMG_F_READONLY, diskimg.c:975) and INT 21h
# AH=36h reports zero bytes free on it, so the installer can read the disk and
# can write to C:, but nothing can write back to A:.
item: Pyro! (floppy) | /WINDIR/DRIVE_A/INSTALL.EXE | icon=dosapp | type=dos

# 2026-08-26 (no ticket, discentry): THE DIG AND RED ALERT WERE MEASURED AND DELIBERATELY
# HAVE NO LINE HERE. Both were written into the stick's tail chain alongside the
# two Discworld II discs, both load, both run real instructions, and NEITHER
# reaches a playable state. A line for either would be a launcher for a black
# window, which is worse than no line: the existence check in
# startmenu_model.rs::sm_model_finish() cannot save us here, because the paths
# DO exist. This block records what each one actually does so the next person
# does not spend a day re-deriving it.
#
# THE VOLUME ORDER THIS ALL ASSUMES, and the reason it is worth writing down:
# usbvol walks the tail chain in ascending offset order and assigns the lowest
# free CD letter, so chain position IS the drive letter. Written disc1, disc2,
# DIG, RA-Allies, RA-Soviet, the mapping MEASURED on build 2214 is
#   E: Discworld II disc 1   F: Discworld II disc 2   G: THEDIG
#   H: RA CD1 (Allies)       I: RA CD2 (Soviet)
# and "[USBVOL] 5 data volume(s) mounted from the boot device tail". Adding
# three volumes after Discworld II does NOT shift E: or F:, and the Discworld II
# line below was re-verified to its main menu with all five present.
#
# THE DIG (/WINDIR/DRIVE_G/DIG/DIG.EXE). It is a bound DOS/4GW binary: MZ at
# file offset 0, LE at 0x2c90, so le_find_rs() takes it and dosexec runs the LE
# directly rather than its 16-bit extender stub. DO NOT point an entry at
# DOS4GW.EXE the way DIG.BAT does; that file is the extender itself, a plain
# real-mode MZ with no LE in it, and running it would be running the loader
# instead of the game. DIG95.EXE is the Win95 build and there is no Win32
# subsystem (#288).
#   MEASURED: the LE loads, the arena is 32,572 KiB, and the guest dies at
#   77,379 instructions with "Not enough memory to allocate file structures"
#   and INT 21h AH=4Ch exit code 1, having presented 3 frames of nothing.
#   The cause is NOT the CD and NOT the arena size. Watcom's 32-bit runtime
#   grows its heap with INT 21h AH=4Ah on its own flat data segment, and the
#   DOS/4GW bridge routes 4Ah to the REAL-MODE MCB allocator (int21_class() in
#   rustkern/dos4gw.rs classes 0x48/0x49/0x4A as A_REGS, and dosexec.c:1959
#   answers them out of conventional memory). With /CONFIG/DOSDIAG.CFG on, the
#   one line that says it all is
#     [dos] 4Ah resize es=0000 req=cf86 maxpara=a000 top=2000 ss=0000
#   i.e. the guest asked to set its data segment to 0xCF86 paragraphs (850,016
#   bytes) and was refused against a 640 KB ceiling, inside a 32 MB arena.
#   Discworld II survives only because its initial break is already big enough:
#   DWB.EXE contains the identical `mov ah,0xED / int 21h` heap-grow and simply
#   never calls it.
#   THE FIX IS A BRIDGE FEATURE, NOT A MENU LINE: 4Ah under a 32-bit guest means
#   "set the flat data segment's limit", and needs a break region reserved
#   between the top of the loaded image and DOS4GW_HEAP_SLACK's DPMI pool. That
#   is dos4gw work; when it lands, this entry becomes one line and a re-test.
#
# 2026-08-27 (no ticket, rafault) RED ALERT NOW PLAYS, AND THIS FILE FINALLY GETS
# ITS ITEM LINE. Everything from here to the "RED ALERT (/WINDIR/DRIVE_H/..."
# block below is the history of four blockers, all now resolved; the block below
# is still true about the DISCS and is kept for that reason alone.
#
#   RESOLVED, install (rawrite): /DOS/RA ships GAME.DAT, REDALERT.MIX and
#   REDALERT.INI on the asset base, root:root 0644 under a 0755 directory, with
#   RA.EXE deliberately absent because it is the 1,656-byte stub, not the game.
#   Guest writes go to a per-user overlay at <home>/GAMES/RA, which is the only
#   design that can work here: the DOS/4G swap file's name is EIGHT RANDOM
#   CHARACTERS chosen per run, so there is no filename a permission row could
#   name.
#   RESOLVED, memory (ra4gw): INT 21h AH=4Ah on the guest's own flat selector is
#   answered from the DPMI arena, not the 640 KB MCB allocator.
#   RESOLVED, mouse (raplay): DPMI 0200h no longer answers an IRET byte for
#   vector 33h, so the driver-detect test succeeds, and the INT 33h 0Ch event
#   handler installed through a DPMI 0300h reflection is now actually called.
#   RESOLVED, the derail (rafault): the guest was not drawing black frames on
#   purpose and it was not stuck. A successful INT 21h AH=4Eh copied 128 bytes
#   (the SIZE of the bridge's DTA mirror) back into the guest's DTA when a find
#   result is 43 bytes, so the volume-label find that finds the disc wrote 85
#   bytes of nothing over Red Alert's own stack - its saved registers at DTA+0x30,
#   its saved EBP at DTA+0x3c and ITS RETURN ADDRESS at DTA+0x40. It then
#   returned to guest linear 0 with every GPR zero. rustkern/dos4gw.rs now copies
#   DTA_FIND_LEN = 43, and the 4GW boot self-test has a poison check that goes
#   RED if that is ever widened again.
#
# MEASURED on golden 2239 (this build's own kernel, no diagnostic overrides),
# with the Allies disc mounted on H: as an ISO 9660 image: Red Alert plays its
# full VQA intro - "Trinity, New Mexico", the laboratory, the Westwood Studios
# logo, the Red Alert title - and reaches Allied mission 1 in game, with the
# sidebar, the objective line, map scroll, a moving cursor and unit tooltips.
# Consecutive free-running frames differ by 64-75%. Zero MISS lines.
#
# AND MEASURED WITH NO DISC AT ALL, which is what decides the item line: Red
# Alert draws its OWN dialog, "PLEASE INSERT A RED ALERT CD INTO THE CD-ROM
# DRIVE", with working OK/CANCEL buttons and a live mouse cursor. That is a
# legible message from the game about what is missing, not the black window this
# file exists to prevent, so the entry is honest on a machine with no disc.
#
# WHAT THE USER STILL HAS TO PROVIDE: the disc. Put CD1 (or CD2) in the boot
# medium's tail with tools/usbvol/mkusbvol.sh and it is mounted on a CD drive
# letter at boot; Red Alert finds it by VOLUME LABEL ("CD1"/"CD2"), not by drive
# letter, so any letter will do. There is no requires= attribute in this grammar,
# so the item cannot be hidden when no disc is present - hence the paragraph
# above being a measurement rather than an assumption.
item: Red Alert | /DOS/RA/GAME.DAT | icon=dosapp | type=dos
#
# RED ALERT (/WINDIR/DRIVE_H/... and /WINDIR/DRIVE_I/...). It refuses to run
# from a CD BY ITS OWN DESIGN, and it refuses twice, so there is no launch line
# on either disc that can work.
#   /INSTALL/RA.EXE is a 1,656-byte real-mode MZ launcher, not the game. At
#   offset 0x37 of its image it does `xor dl,dl / mov ah,36h / int 21h`, i.e.
#   free space on the DEFAULT drive, and a mounted disc correctly reports zero
#   free (int21svc.c:1049 returns BX=0 for a read-only image, which is the truth
#   and not a placeholder). MEASURED: "Error - insufficient disk space to run
#   Red Alert. Red Alert requires 8,388,608 bytes of disk space." and it sits on
#   "Press a key to continue."
#   /INSTALL/GAME.DAT is the actual game, a Rational DOS/4G LE (inner MZ at file
#   offset 233,508, LE at 244,900). Launching it directly skips the stub and
#   hits the game's OWN copy of the same check: "Insufficient Disk Space to run
#   Red Alert. You must have 8 megabytes of free disk space."
#   The Allies and Soviet discs are byte-identical in layout, so this is one
#   game with two data discs, not two games: whichever way it eventually runs it
#   wants ONE entry, not two.
#   HOW FAR IT GETS WHEN THAT CHECK PASSES, measured by copying GAME.DAT +
#   REDALERT.MIX + REDALERT.INI to a writable C: (which reports 268 MB free) and
#   launching /DOS/RA/GAME.DAT: the disk check passes, it chdirs, reads
#   REDALERT.INI, hooks INT 08h, sets mode 13h, and then loops forever on
#     [GUESTFS-DENY] ... op=INT21/3Ch create ... path=/DOS/RA/./REDALERT.INI
#   because /DOS/<GAME> falls into perms_check()'s root-owned 0755 no-entry
#   default and the guest runs as the uid-1000 desktop session. That is the same
#   wall NetHack and SimCity hit, and fs/perms.c:333-405 already records the
#   answer: an explicit entry for the file the game writes, not a writable game
#   directory. Beyond that it still needs MAIN.MIX from the disc and it will
#   reach the same AH=4Ah heap-grow gap The Dig found.
#   So Red Alert needs an INSTALLED tree plus a perms entry plus the bridge fix.
#   None of those is a Start-menu change, and until all three exist there is
#   nothing here to launch.
